{:edb/db-before-t 2312 :edb/db-after-t 2313 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535625 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:52:57.886Z" 13194139535625 true] [17592186119455 1100 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186119455 1100 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j$.\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$." 13194139535625 false] [17592186119455 1145 "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin ti - \\cos tj) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i - \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti - (-\\sin t)j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + \\sin tj \\\\\nT^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4})i + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4})j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i + j‖ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(‖T^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})‖) = (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j))/(1) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\frac{π}{4}) \\cdot N(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 false] [17592186119455 1145 "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186119456 1100 "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." 13194139535625 true] [17592186119456 1100 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t, \\: \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle$, find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." 13194139535625 false] [17592186119456 1145 "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$. To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{3}t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\sin t, \\cos t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 + \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= (r^{′}(t))/(‖r^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\sin t, \\cos t⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}, \\frac{\\cos t}{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t)$. To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{\\cos t}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos t,-\\frac{1}{2}\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(t)‖ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{0^{2} + (-\\cos t)^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) &= (T^{′}(t))/(‖T^{′}(t)‖) = (\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩)/((\\frac{1}{2})) = ⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) \\cdot N(t) &= ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\sin t}{2}, \\frac{\\cos t}{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0,-\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 0 + \\frac{\\sin t\\cos t}{2} - \\frac{\\cos t\\sin t}{2} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 false] [17592186119456 1145 "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186119457 1100 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186119457 1100 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t)$.\nFind the principal normal vector 