rac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186358179 1113 #uuid "c8c3f8ef-4d49-5c32-a539-c6eef2f1ab59" 13194139535625 true] [17592186358179 1145 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535625 true] [17592186358180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358181 13194139535625 